01:08:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Exactly! You’ve put your finger on one of the most wonderful and ear‑opening aspects of music-making. Those aren’t just music‑theory trivia—they’re **audible, on‑purpose adjustments** that give a live performance its “in‑tune” glow. Let’s make that tangible. Imagine a violinist playing a double‑stop: they’re bowing an open E string along with a C♯ on the A string, forming an A‑major third (A–C♯). If they stop the C♯ so that the fifth with the open A is perfectly pure (a 3:2 ratio), the C♯ will be **14 cents flat** compared to the equal‑tempered piano. That’s a noticeably mellower, “sweeter” sound—the overtones lock in. That’s the C♯ as a **harmonically pure major third**. Now the same violinist, a moment later, plays a D♭ major chord (D♭–F–A♭). The D♭ here is the root, and to make the octaves and fifths ring pure against other open strings or other players, they’ll place it slightly differently. If they try to use that same “low C♯” pitch from the A‑major chord, the whole D♭ chord will sound flat and lifeless. So they nudge it up by that comma—the same 21.5 cents we mentioned—and the harmonic blend clears up. You can literally practice this with a drone and your own voice or a fretless instrument. Sing a steady drone note, then sing a C♯ against it as a leading tone (bright, rising), then hold the drone and sing a D♭ as a stable root. Your ear will naturally want to place them slightly apart. The difference isn’t just an intellectual label; it’s a **physical sensation** of tension and resolution. So when you hear a great string quartet or a capella vocal group, they aren’t just playing “the notes.” They’re constantly sliding those half‑steps by a few invisible cents depending on whether the note is a third, a leading tone, a perfect fifth in an inverted chord, etc. That’s what makes the harmony “bloom” in a way a perfectly etched equal‑temperament grid never can. Your initial hunch was correct: there’s a real physical difference in Hertz,